From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Sun, 18 Sep 94 19:53:46 WET DST
Subject: [#94-9-257] Quantum Erasers? (fwd)

   From what I recall of quantum erasers, the correct interpretation
is that they perform a standard two-slit experiment. In the
two slit experiment, you will get an interference pattern as
long as the information about which slit a photon went through
is unknown. If one places a photodetector at either slit, the
pattern disappears. The quantum eraser experiment reestablishes
the pattern by erasing the measurement and knowledge of the
measurement from the observers. The goal is to show that it is
the knowledge of the observer which effects the experiment outcome.


-Ray


